1. sk-a #24 / sk-b #15 are attributed to "round-4 layout damage" on the Slovak side. They are not: the Korean source
   carries the same missing `##` and the same U+200B lines (verified byte-for-byte counts, above). They are a
   source-side issue for every locale, not a Slovak defect.
2. sk-a #23 / sk-b #4 (space thousands separator) are correct about Slovak orthography but are ruled out by the
   campaign's own checker profile (R14). Standing supervisor decision, not a reviewer error as such — noted so the
   next round does not re-file them.
3. sk-a #14's suggested rewrite uses "odporúčanie" twice in one list ("…, odporúčanie, odporúčanie zo strany umelej
   inteligencie ani…"). Applied the intent with *schválenie* for "endorsement" instead.
4. sk-a #11's suggested FAQ title would have read "konzultácia v slovenčine", which trips the checker's own
   `sk-consult-lang` forbidden-claim regex. Used "Je možná konzultácia v slovenskom jazyku?" instead; the answer is
   still a flat "Nie."
5. sk-a #1 counts 13 "prieskum" in 001; the file had 12 (plus 1 in 002). All administrative/bank senses were changed;
   018 L92 "vo fáze prieskumu trhu" is a genuine market survey and was deliberately left.
6. sk-a #7's example "u ostatných zamestnancov" (008 L179) is not the flagged pattern — *u* + person is standard
   Slovak. Only *u* + institution/abstract noun was changed.
7. sk-b #20 lists "obdržať rôzne požiadavky" and "po obdržaní" in 018 — correct; but sk-b #21's "učinil" in 003/007
   does not exist: those hits are "zadostiučinenie", a different lexeme.
8. Not a reviewer error, but recorded: the checker's `neighbour` check reports "L43: zda" in 001 and three lines in 004.
   It is a false positive — JavaScript's `\b` is ASCII-only, so "zdaňujú" matches /\bzda\b/. Pre-existing (reproduced
   against `git show HEAD:…`), WARN only, no Czech word is present.

